# abstract-name = concrete package(s) on this base
# Browser and audio firmware come from upstream forks (mod 05) until packages/ ships;
# the acceptance policy forbids the stock firefox and firmware-sof-signed packages.
browser            =
# Ubuntu's own Firefox package is named plain "firefox" — there is no
# separate "firefox-esr" here, that is Debian's own naming (see
# bases/debian/packages.map) — and "firefox" itself is on this engine's own
# forbidden-image-package list (tests/assertions/install.py's
# FORBIDDEN_IMAGE_PACKAGES) because the browser role above already ships
# Firefox from the packages/firefox-synos fork (mod 05) instead of the
# stock package. Checked against archive.ubuntu.com's real indexes for
# noble and resolute, including -updates/-backports/-security, 2026-09-25:
# no "firefox-esr" package or Provides anywhere. Currently unreferenced by
# any profile or bundle; marked unavailable rather than left pointing at a
# name this base's archive does not have.
browser-esr        = unavailable: Ubuntu ships no firefox-esr package (Debian-only naming) and its own plain "firefox" is on this project's own forbidden-image-package list in favor of the packages/firefox-synos fork the browser role above already provides
office             = libreoffice libreoffice-gtk4
mail               = evolution evolution-ews
firmware-audio     =
firmware-wifi      = linux-firmware
# ${LANG} here does not mean "substitute the language code literally" —
# hunspell-de does not exist (the real name is hunspell-de-de) and Irish's
# own language-pack exists on noble but not on resolute. resolve_packages()
# resolves ${LANG} through bases/ubuntu/language-packages.map
# (tools/generate_language_packages.py), the real, archive-verified name
# (or a documented "this language has nothing for this role" fact, per
# suite where it differs) for every language this engine can be asked for,
# not this line's own literal template.
translations       = language-pack-${LANG} language-pack-${LANG}-base language-pack-gnome-${LANG} language-pack-gnome-${LANG}-base
spellcheck         = hunspell-${LANG}
# The desktop is provided by the upstream metapackage (mod 05) until packages/ ships.
gnome-desktop      =
rdp-client         = remmina remmina-plugin-rdp
vpn                = network-manager-openvpn-gnome wireguard-tools
printing           = cups printer-driver-all
smartcard          = opensc pcscd
directory          = sssd realmd adcli krb5-user libpam-sss libnss-sss oddjob-mkhomedir
hardening          = apparmor-profiles-extra ufw auditd fwupd unattended-upgrades
usbguard           = usbguard
# libopenscap's SONAME/soversion differs by suite, not merely by base:
# checked against archive.ubuntu.com's real indexes, including
# -updates/-backports/-security, 2026-09-25 — noble carries libopenscap25t64,
# resolute (a newer archive) already carries libopenscap33; "libopenscap8"
# is on neither. No single name is right for every suite this base
# supports, so this is a per-suite override (see load_package_map's
# `abstract-name@suite` syntax); a caller that resolves without a suite
# refuses loudly instead of guessing which one to use.
openscap           = unavailable: libopenscap's SONAME differs per suite (libopenscap25t64 on noble, libopenscap33 on resolute) — resolve with a suite so this file's openscap@noble / openscap@resolute override applies
openscap@noble     = libopenscap25t64 ssg-base
openscap@resolute  = libopenscap33 ssg-base
containers         = podman buildah skopeo
evince             = evince
gnome-calendar     = gnome-calendar
build-essential    = build-essential
gdb                = gdb
cmake              = cmake
# VS Code OSS/Codium is not packaged in Ubuntu's own archive at all —
# checked against archive.ubuntu.com's real indexes for noble and resolute,
# including -updates/-backports/-security, 2026-09-25: no "code",
# "code-oss", "codium" or "vscodium" package, and no Provides for any of
# them, on either suite. It ships only from Microsoft's own apt repository
# (proprietary VS Code) or third-party repositories this engine does not
# add by default — the same shape as browser-headless's Chromium gap
# below, and the same question Google Chrome already settled for the
# browser role: this project does not repackage proprietary software, and
# it has nothing free to substitute here either. Marked unavailable rather
# than silently resolving to Microsoft's proprietary "code" package.
code-oss           = unavailable: not packaged in Ubuntu's own archive on any supported suite; ships only from Microsoft's own (proprietary VS Code) or third-party apt repositories, which this engine does not add by default
ssh-server         = openssh-server
web-admin          = cockpit cockpit-podman cockpit-networkmanager cockpit-storaged
virtualization     = qemu-system libvirt-daemon-system virtinst
gpu-nvidia         = nvidia-driver-580-open nvidia-cuda-toolkit nvidia-utils-580
gpu-amd            = rocminfo rocm-smi
gpu-intel          = intel-opencl-icd libze-intel-gpu1 libze1 intel-media-va-driver-non-free
ai-dev             = python3-torch python3-venv python3-pip llama.cpp
# python3-torch and llama.cpp are real, installable packages on resolute
# (checked against archive.ubuntu.com's real indexes, including
# -updates/-backports/-security, 2026-09-25) but do not exist anywhere in
# noble's own archive at all — not renamed, not split, genuinely absent;
# PyTorch/llama.cpp packaging landed in Debian/Ubuntu after noble's own
# release. The AI workstation appliance (bundle-catalog/ai-workstation,
# the ai-dev bundle above) needs both, so it is pinned to suite resolute
# instead of this engine's usual noble (see its own manifest.yml).
ai-dev@noble       = unavailable: python3-torch and llama.cpp are not packaged for Ubuntu noble at all (checked against archive.ubuntu.com's real indexes, including noble-updates/-backports/-security, 2026-09-25); both are present on resolute's own archive instead — pin suite resolute (or base debian/trixie) for a profile that needs this role
# Yocto Project reference manual, "Ubuntu and Debian" required packages, plus
# python3-websockets (needed on every supported suite but jammy/22.04, where
# the archive version is too old for BitBake's hash equivalence server; use
# a venv there instead). lz4, not the transitional liblz4-tool.
yocto-host-tools   = gawk wget git diffstat unzip texinfo build-essential chrpath socat cpio python3 python3-pip python3-pexpect xz-utils debianutils iputils-ping python3-git python3-jinja2 python3-subunit zstd lz4 file locales libacl1 libcrypt-dev gcc python3-websockets
# Practical build-host tools the manual doesn't list but a real Yocto host
# needs day to day; small enough to ship by default. Only the x86 and arm
# system emulators plus qemu-utils (not the qemu-system metapackage, which
# also pulls mips/ppc/sparc/s390x emulators for roughly 470 MB more).
yocto-practical-tools = rsync bc bison flex libssl-dev git-lfs bmap-tools pigz ccache device-tree-compiler u-boot-tools qemu-system-x86 qemu-system-arm qemu-utils
# Building the Yocto Project documentation, including PDFs (texlive is large:
# this is its own bundle so a person opts in rather than always paying for it).
yocto-doc-tools    = make librsvg2-bin python3-saneyaml python3-venv fonts-freefont-otf latexmk tex-gyre texlive-fonts-extra texlive-fonts-recommended texlive-lang-all texlive-latex-extra texlive-latex-recommended texlive-xetex
# The "test-engine" bundle (profiles/bundles.yml): everything
# packaging/install-test-engine.sh's REQUIREMENTS list installs by hand on a
# spare machine, checked here so a profile can ship the same tooling inside
# the image itself — the container runtime tools/bundle_launcher.sh builds
# in, QEMU and xorriso for the ISO boot smoke test, Tesseract and Pillow for
# its OCR check, and PyYAML for tools/catalog_conformance.py itself
# (docs/BUILD_MATRIX.md, "Installing it as a service"). python3 is listed
# explicitly because this bundle must also work on a profile with no
# desktop, where nothing else already pulls it in.
python3            = python3
pyyaml             = python3-yaml
qemu               = qemu-system-x86
xorriso            = xorriso
tesseract          = tesseract-ocr
pillow             = python3-pil
# Headless-capable Chromium/Chrome, the way tools/devtools_browser.py drives
# it (google-chrome, google-chrome-stable, chromium-browser or chromium).
# Genuinely unavailable on this base, checked against the archives, not
# assumed: archive.ubuntu.com's Packages indexes for jammy, noble and
# resolute all show "chromium"/"chromium-browser" as a transitional package
# (Pre-Depends: debconf, snapd) whose only job is installing the Chromium
# *snap* — this base ships no snapd at all (test_package_policy.py,
# FORBIDDEN_IMAGE_PACKAGES), so apt-get installing it would either drag
# snapd onto the image or fail outright. Unlike Firefox (packages/firefox-synos,
# forked from Mozilla's own repository — MPL-licensed, meant to be
# redistributed), Google Chrome is proprietary: its terms do not allow a
# third party to repackage and redistribute it the way a fork.json recipe
# would (test_package_policy.py's ForkSourceAllowListTests enforces this).
# So: no substitute, marked unavailable rather than silently dropped —
# resolve_packages() refuses a profile that reaches this name on this base
# instead of quietly shipping one package short (tools/render_manifest.py).
browser-headless   = unavailable: Ubuntu packages no non-snap, freely redistributable Chromium (every "chromium"/"chromium-browser" is transitional, Pre-Depends snapd, and this engine ships no snapd); build the "test-engine" bundle on debian instead, whose own archive ships a real, native, BSD-licensed Chromium
# Bundle-catalog workstation appliances (docs/BUNDLE.md, "The bundle
# catalog"): each catalogued bundle pins one base and one suite in its own
# manifest, so a package below only has to exist on that entry's suite,
# checked with rmadison against that suite specifically (noted per group);
# it need not exist on every suite this base supports.
# audio-workstation targets noble: PipeWire's JACK compatibility layer
# (pipewire-jack, qpwgraph) is on noble/resolute but missing from jammy,
# so a jammy-based derived profile using this group would need jackd2/
# qjackctl instead.
audio-production-tools = ardour pipewire-jack qpwgraph a2jmidid jack-mixer calf-plugins x42-plugins lsp-plugins-jack hydrogen guitarix rakarrack rtkit
# video-editing-workstation targets noble.
video-editing-tools    = kdenlive ffmpeg va-driver-all vainfo mesa-va-drivers vdpau-driver-all
# photography-workstation targets noble (displaycal is missing from jammy).
photography-tools      = darktable rawtherapee argyll gphoto2 displaycal
# cad-3d-printing-workstation targets Debian trixie instead (see
# bases/debian/packages.map): FreeCAD and Cura are missing from noble and
# resolute, so the Ubuntu side of this group stays the OpenSCAD/PrusaSlicer
# set, which is available on every Ubuntu suite this engine supports.
cad-3d-printing-tools  = openscad prusa-slicer
data-science-tools     = jupyter-notebook python3-numpy python3-scipy python3-pandas python3-matplotlib python3-sklearn python3-seaborn
security-research-tools = nmap wireshark tcpdump aircrack-ng sqlmap nikto hydra john hashcat gobuster whatweb dirb netcat-openbsd libcap2-bin
# retro-gaming-console targets noble, which also carries Nestopia (NES) and
# Beetle PCE Fast (PC Engine/TurboGrafx-16); Beetle PCE Fast is missing
# from Debian trixie, so it is Ubuntu-only (see bases/debian/packages.map).
retro-gaming-tools     = retroarch retroarch-assets joystick jstest-gtk libretro-snes9x libretro-mgba libretro-gambatte libretro-desmume libretro-nestopia libretro-beetle-pce-fast
